  "raw_response": "{\n  \"title\": \"FVG Mitigation Example: Price pulls back to fill unmitigated FVG below major swing low, not signaling reversal\",\n  \"school\": \"Smart Money Concepts (SMC)\",\n  \"asset\": \"EURUSD\",\n  \"timeframe\": \"1H\",\n  \"market_context\": \"Uptrend with imperfect structure; price formed a major swing low (origin of BOS), then later pulled back toward an unmitigated fair value gap located below that major swing low. The analyst emphasizes this pullback is not a reversal but gap-filling behavior.\",\n  \"key_observation\": \"An unmitigated FVG exists directly below a major swing low; when price returns to fill that FVG, it does not constitute a valid change of character or trend reversal.\",\n  \"analysis_steps\": [\n    \"Step 1: Identify a major swing low as the origin of a valid break of structure (BOS) in an uptrend.\",\n    \"Step 2: Observe an unmitigated Fair Value Gap (FVG) located below that major swing low on the left side of the chart.\",\n    \"Step 3: Note that price pulls back toward the FVG zone—not breaking below the major swing low—to fill the imbalance/inefficiency.\",\n    \"Step 4: Conclude this FVG mitigation does not invalidate the uptrend or signal a true reversal, thus avoiding false short signals.\"\n  ],\n  \"outcome\": null,\n  \"lessons\": \"Always check for unmitigated FVGs below key swing points before interpreting a break below as a reversal; price often returns to fill imbalances without changing trend direction.\",\n  \"primary_image_frame_id\": \"5-pQbpg1Z0Q_f028\",\n  \"supporting_image_f",
